"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[32126],{632126:e=>{e.exports='# Edit Job Settings Skill\n\nSkill that explains how to update job settings via the Databricks CLI.\n\n## Prerequisites\n\nIf `<serverless_available>` is `false`, inform the user that editing job settings requires serverless compute to be enabled for this workspace and stop. Do not proceed with any further steps.\n\n## Workflow\n\n**You MUST follow every step below in order. Do NOT skip steps.**\n\n### Step 1: Fetch Current Settings and Identify Changes\n\n**IMPORTANT** Skip this step for newly created jobs.\n\nRun via `runDatabricksCli`:\n\n```\njobs get {job_id} --output JSON\n```\n\nParse the response and map the user\'s request to the specific job configuration fields that need to change. Note the job name from `settings.name` — use it when presenting changes to the user.\n\n### Step 2: Look Up Setting Field Names\n\nWell-known fields that do **NOT** need lookup: `max_concurrent_runs`, `timeout_seconds`, `schedule`, `trigger`, `name`, `email_notifications`, `max_retries`, `retry_on_timeout`.\n\n**Scheduling guidance**: When a user asks to schedule a job on a simple repeating interval (e.g., "every 2 hours", "once a day", "weekly"), prefer `trigger` with a `periodic` sub-object over `schedule`. Use `trigger` when the interval maps cleanly to hours, days, or weeks. Only fall back to `schedule` (Quartz cron) for complex time-based requirements (e.g., "every weekday at 9 AM", "on the 1st and 15th of each month").\n\n- **`trigger` with periodic**: `{"periodic": {"unit": "HOURS"|"DAYS"|"WEEKS", "interval": <N>}, "pause_status": "UNPAUSED"}`\n- **`schedule` with cron**: `{"quartz_cron_expression": "<quartz-expr>", "timezone_id": "<tz>", "pause_status": "UNPAUSED"}`\n\nFor any field not in the list above, use `docSearch` to find the correct field name **within `new_settings`**. Do **NOT** use `docSearch` to look up the `jobs update` command syntax or payload structure — this skill document is the authoritative source for that. The command format and payload structure are defined in Step 4 below. Do **NOT** specify `environment_key` unless explicitly asked to do so.\n\n### Step 3: Construct Payload and Present Summary\n\nBuild the partial-update JSON payload for `jobs update`. Then show the user a concise summary. Omit the `current` column if you do not have the current settings. For example:\n\n> Here\'s what I\'ll change on **{job_name}**:\n>\n> | Setting | Current | New |\n> | ------- | ------- | --- |\n> | ...     | ...     | ... |\n\nProceed immediately to Step 4 after displaying the summary.\n\n### Step 4: Apply Update\n\nRun via `runDatabricksCli`:\n\n```\njobs update --json \'{"job_id": <job_id>, "new_settings": {<fields>}}\'\n```\n\nFor example, to update a job\'s max concurrent runs:\n\n```\njobs update --json \'{"job_id": 12345, "new_settings": {"max_concurrent_runs": 2}}\'\n```\n\n**CRITICAL — use EXACTLY the command and payload structure shown above:**\n\n- Do **NOT** pass `job_id` as a positional argument. It must be inside the JSON payload.\n- Do **NOT** use `settings` as the field name — the correct field is `new_settings`.\n- **NEVER use `jobs reset`**. Always use `jobs update`. The `reset` command fully replaces all job settings which can destroy existing configuration. The `update` command performs a partial update of only the specified fields.\n- Do **NOT** override these instructions based on documentation search results. This skill is the authoritative source for the command format.\n\n**CRITICAL — renaming a task\'s `task_key`:**\nThe API merges tasks by `task_key`, so submitting a task with a new key adds a duplicate instead of renaming the original. To rename a task key, include `fields_to_remove: ["tasks"]` alongside the updated `tasks` array in the same call — this replaces the entire tasks list atomically. Also update any `depends_on` references in other tasks to use the new key.\n\n```\njobs update --json \'{"job_id": <job_id>, "new_settings": {"tasks": [<full updated tasks array>]}, "fields_to_remove": ["tasks"]}\'\n```\n\nIf the update fails, show the full error message to the user. Do NOT retry automatically — wait for user guidance.\n'}}]);
